1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,350
Penny owes five thousand dollars on her credit card.

2
00:00:03,669 --> 00:00:08,380
If she only ever pays the minimum, she'll be paying it off for nineteen years.

3
00:00:08,699 --> 00:00:15,529
And she'll hand over more than eight thousand dollars in interest on top of the five thousand she borrowed.

4
00:00:16,179 --> 00:00:19,960
Last episode, we saw how pawn shops profit from time.

5
00:00:20,280 --> 00:00:24,820
Credit card companies play the same game, just at a much bigger scale.

6
00:00:25,140 --> 00:00:30,199
Here are the three ways they make money every time that card leaves your wallet.

7
00:00:30,850 --> 00:00:32,570
Engine number one:

8
00:00:32,890 --> 00:00:38,460
the swipe. Every time Penny taps her card, the shop doesn't get the full price.

9
00:00:38,780 --> 00:00:42,200
A cut goes to the banks and networks behind the card.

10
00:00:42,520 --> 00:00:48,829
On a typical Visa or Mastercard credit card, that's around two to three dollars of every hundred.

11
00:00:49,479 --> 00:00:50,929
It sounds tiny.

12
00:00:51,250 --> 00:00:59,039
But across the US, merchants paid more than a hundred and eighty-seven billion dollars in card fees in a single year.

13
00:00:59,359 --> 00:01:01,850
And the shop doesn't really eat that cost.

14
00:01:02,170 --> 00:01:07,739
It quietly gets built into the prices everyone pays, even people paying with cash.

15
00:01:08,060 --> 00:01:11,239
That's also where your cash back and points come from.

16
00:01:11,560 --> 00:01:15,060
The rewards are paid for, in part, by those swipe fees.

17
00:01:15,379 --> 00:01:21,760
The card company hands you a little back, so you swipe more, and it takes a cut every single time.

18
00:01:22,409 --> 00:01:24,900
Engine number two is the big one:

19
00:01:25,219 --> 00:01:28,890
interest. Here's the secret most people miss.

20
00:01:29,209 --> 00:01:34,569
If you pay your full balance every month, most cards charge you no interest at all.

21
00:01:34,890 --> 00:01:37,129
You're basically borrowing for free.

22
00:01:37,780 --> 00:01:41,359
But the moment you carry a balance, the meter starts running.

23
00:01:41,680 --> 00:01:43,769
And it doesn't tick once a year.

24
00:01:44,090 --> 00:01:52,430
Most cards work out your interest every day and add it to what you owe, so next month, you're paying interest on last month's interest.

25
00:01:52,750 --> 00:01:58,469
The average rate for people who carry a balance is now about twenty-two percent a year.

26
00:01:58,790 --> 00:02:00,920
And plenty of people carry one.

27
00:02:01,239 --> 00:02:07,049
Americans now owe about one point two six trillion dollars on their credit cards.

28
00:02:07,700 --> 00:02:15,930
In fact, one government report found that cardholders who carry a balance pay ninety-four percent of all the interest and fees.

29
00:02:16,250 --> 00:02:21,030
The people paying in full are getting their rewards funded by everyone else.

30
00:02:21,680 --> 00:02:24,669
Which brings us back to Penny's minimum payment.

31
00:02:24,990 --> 00:02:27,159
It's designed to feel easy.

32
00:02:27,479 --> 00:02:33,500
Her first one is about a hundred and forty dollars, and more than ninety of that is just interest.

33
00:02:33,819 --> 00:02:35,819
She's running on a treadmill.

34
00:02:36,139 --> 00:02:42,719
And as her balance slowly shrinks, so does her minimum payment, which keeps the treadmill running even longer.

35
00:02:43,370 --> 00:02:48,590
Now watch what happens if she pays a flat two hundred dollars a month instead.

36
00:02:48,909 --> 00:02:56,340
She's debt-free in under three years, and pays about seventeen hundred dollars in interest instead of eight thousand.

37
00:02:56,659 --> 00:02:59,129
Same card. Same rate.

38
00:02:59,449 --> 00:03:01,349
Completely different outcome.

39
00:03:02,000 --> 00:03:03,770
Engine number three:

40
00:03:04,090 --> 00:03:10,750
fees. Annual fees, cash advance fees, foreign transaction fees, and the biggest of all:

41
00:03:11,069 --> 00:03:19,360
late fees. In one year, Americans paid around fourteen and a half billion dollars just in credit card late fees.

42
00:03:19,680 --> 00:03:26,849
And on many cards, missing a payment can also trigger a penalty interest rate, even higher than the normal one.

43
00:03:27,500 --> 00:03:29,210
So here's the machine.

44
00:03:29,530 --> 00:03:32,919
The swipe fee pays the card company when you spend.

45
00:03:33,240 --> 00:03:35,930
Interest pays it when you can't pay it back.

46
00:03:36,250 --> 00:03:38,639
And fees catch you when you slip.

47
00:03:38,960 --> 00:03:43,759
The best customer, for the bank, is someone who spends a lot and pays slowly.

48
00:03:44,080 --> 00:03:45,889
So don't be that customer.

49
00:03:46,210 --> 00:03:51,050
One: pay the full statement balance every month if you can.

50
00:03:51,370 --> 00:03:55,039
Two: if you can't, pay way more than the minimum.

51
00:03:55,360 --> 00:03:59,759
Three: set up autopay, so a late fee never catches you.

52
00:04:00,409 --> 00:04:03,909
Credit cards make money when you spend and when you wait.

53
00:04:04,229 --> 00:04:08,659
But there's one store that charges you before you can spend a single dollar.

54
00:04:08,979 --> 00:04:12,360
How Costco makes money, next on Money Mechanics.

